-- ============================================================
-- IT Infrastructure and Architecture (14 questions)
-- ============================================================

(5, 'IT Infrastructure and Architecture',
 'Which of the following best describes the role of a hypervisor in a virtualized IT environment?',
 '["It encrypts data transmitted between servers", "It manages and allocates physical hardware resources to virtual machines", "It provides firewall protection at the network perimeter", "It stores backup copies of virtual machine images"]'::jsonb,
 1,
 'A hypervisor (also called a virtual machine monitor) is software that creates and manages virtual machines by allocating physical hardware resources — CPU, memory, storage, and network — to each VM. It enables multiple operating systems to run on a single physical host, improving resource utilization and flexibility.',
 'easy'),

(5, 'IT Infrastructure and Architecture',
 'An organization is evaluating cloud service models. Which model provides the MOST control over the operating system and middleware layers?',
 '["Software as a Service (SaaS)", "Platform as a Service (PaaS)", "Infrastructure as a Service (IaaS)", "Function as a Service (FaaS)"]'::jsonb,
 2,
 'IaaS provides virtualized computing resources (servers, storage, networking) where the customer manages the OS, middleware, runtime, and applications. PaaS manages the OS and middleware for you, SaaS manages the entire stack, and FaaS (serverless) abstracts away even more infrastructure.',
 'easy'),

(5, 'IT Infrastructure and Architecture',
 'A CPA firm is advising a client on network architecture. The client wants to isolate its accounting servers from the general employee network while still allowing controlled access. Which network design is most appropriate?',
 '["Single flat network with host-based firewalls", "Demilitarized zone (DMZ) with public-facing servers", "Network segmentation using VLANs with access control lists", "Air-gapped network with no external connectivity"]'::jsonb,
 2,
 'VLANs (Virtual Local Area Networks) with ACLs provide network segmentation that isolates the accounting servers logically while still allowing controlled access through defined rules. A flat network offers no isolation, a DMZ is for public-facing servers, and an air gap is too restrictive for internal users who need controlled access.',
 'medium'),

(5, 'IT Infrastructure and Architecture',
 'Which of the following is a characteristic of a private cloud deployment model compared to a public cloud?',
 '["Lower upfront capital expenditure", "Shared multi-tenant infrastructure", "Greater control over data residency and compliance", "Pay-per-use pricing with no committed capacity"]'::jsonb,
 2,
 'Private clouds provide dedicated infrastructure for a single organization, offering greater control over data residency, security configurations, and regulatory compliance. Public clouds offer lower upfront costs and shared infrastructure, but the organization has less control over where data physically resides.',
 'easy'),

(5, 'IT Infrastructure and Architecture',
 'A company uses a three-tier architecture for its financial reporting application. Which tier is responsible for enforcing business rules such as revenue recognition calculations?',
 '["Presentation tier", "Application (logic) tier", "Data tier", "Network tier"]'::jsonb,
 1,
 'In a three-tier architecture, the application (logic/middle) tier processes business rules and logic, such as revenue recognition calculations. The presentation tier handles the user interface, and the data tier manages storage and retrieval of data.',
 'medium'),

(5, 'IT Infrastructure and Architecture',
 'An auditor discovers that a client''s production database and development database reside on the same physical server with no resource isolation. What is the PRIMARY risk?',
 '["Development queries may corrupt production data", "Development staff may gain unauthorized read access to production financial data", "The production database may experience performance degradation from development workloads", "Backup schedules will conflict between environments"]'::jsonb,
 2,
 'While all options present some risk, the primary concern is performance degradation — resource-intensive development queries and testing can consume CPU, memory, and I/O, directly impacting the availability and responsiveness of the production financial reporting system. Proper environment separation is a fundamental IT general control.',
 'medium'),

(5, 'IT Infrastructure and Architecture',
 'Which of the following technologies would best address an organization''s need to ensure that only authorized and properly configured devices can connect to its corporate network?',
 '["Network Address Translation (NAT)", "Network Access Control (NAC)", "Content Delivery Network (CDN)", "Virtual Private Network (VPN)"]'::jsonb,
 1,
 'Network Access Control (NAC) evaluates devices attempting to connect to the network, verifying their identity, security posture (antivirus status, patch level, configuration), and authorization before granting access. NAT translates IP addresses, CDN distributes content, and VPN encrypts remote connections — none enforce device compliance.',
 'medium'),

(5, 'IT Infrastructure and Architecture',
 'During an IT audit, the auditor notes that the client uses a Storage Area Network (SAN) for its financial databases. Which of the following is an advantage of SAN over direct-attached storage?',
 '["Lower implementation cost", "Simplified management with no specialized skills required", "Centralized storage management with high availability and scalability", "Elimination of the need for backup procedures"]'::jsonb,
 2,
 'SANs provide centralized storage management, high availability through redundancy, and easy scalability by adding storage without disrupting servers. SANs are more expensive and complex than direct-attached storage, and they do not eliminate backup requirements.',
 'medium'),

(5, 'IT Infrastructure and Architecture',
 'A multinational company needs to ensure sub-50ms latency for real-time financial transaction processing across its regional offices. Which cloud architecture pattern is most appropriate?',
 '["Single-region deployment with a global CDN", "Multi-region active-active deployment with data replication", "Centralized on-premises data center with VPN tunnels to all offices", "Serverless functions triggered by event queues"]'::jsonb,
 1,
 'A multi-region active-active deployment places compute and data resources close to each regional office, minimizing latency through geographic proximity while maintaining consistency through data replication. A single-region CDN helps with static content but not transaction processing. Centralized on-premises introduces latency for distant offices, and serverless may introduce cold-start latency.',
 'hard'),

(5, 'IT Infrastructure and Architecture',
 'Which of the following best describes a microservices architecture compared to a monolithic architecture?',
 '["All application components are deployed as a single unit", "Each service runs in its own process and communicates via lightweight protocols", "The database is shared across all services for consistency", "Changes to one component require redeployment of the entire application"]'::jsonb,
 1,
 'Microservices architecture decomposes an application into small, independently deployable services that communicate via lightweight protocols (typically REST APIs or message queues). Each service owns its data and can be developed, deployed, and scaled independently. The other options describe monolithic architecture characteristics.',
 'easy'),

(5, 'IT Infrastructure and Architecture',
 'An organization is migrating its general ledger system to the cloud. During the migration, both the legacy on-premises system and the new cloud system will run simultaneously. What is this migration strategy called?',
 '["Big bang migration", "Parallel running", "Phased migration", "Pilot migration"]'::jsonb,
 1,
 'Parallel running (also called parallel operation) involves operating both the old and new systems simultaneously, processing the same transactions in both, and comparing results to verify the new system''s accuracy before decommissioning the old one. Big bang switches all at once, phased migrates in stages by module, and pilot tests with a subset of users.',
 'medium'),

(5, 'IT Infrastructure and Architecture',
 'A company''s disaster recovery plan specifies a Recovery Time Objective (RTO) of 4 hours and a Recovery Point Objective (RPO) of 1 hour for its financial systems. Which infrastructure configuration best meets these requirements?',
 '["Nightly tape backups stored offsite with a cold standby site", "Synchronous replication to a hot standby site with automated failover", "Asynchronous replication to a warm standby site with documented manual failover procedures", "Weekly full backups with daily differential backups to cloud storage"]'::jsonb,
 2,
 'An RPO of 1 hour means no more than 1 hour of data loss is acceptable, and an RTO of 4 hours means systems must be operational within 4 hours. Asynchronous replication to a warm standby site can achieve sub-hour RPO with replication lag, and manual failover within 4 hours is feasible. Synchronous replication with a hot site exceeds the requirements and is more costly. Nightly tape backups (24-hour RPO) and weekly backups far exceed the acceptable data loss.',
 'hard'),

(5, 'IT Infrastructure and Architecture',
 'Which of the following describes the concept of "infrastructure as code" (IaC)?',
 '["Using spreadsheets to document server configurations", "Managing and provisioning infrastructure through machine-readable configuration files rather than manual processes", "Writing custom firmware for network hardware", "Coding applications that run on bare-metal servers"]'::jsonb,
 1,
 'Infrastructure as Code (IaC) is the practice of managing and provisioning computing infrastructure through machine-readable definition files (e.g., Terraform, CloudFormation) rather than physical hardware configuration or interactive configuration tools. It enables version control, repeatability, and automation of infrastructure deployments.',
 'hard'),

(5, 'IT Infrastructure and Architecture',
 'An IT auditor is evaluating a client''s use of containerization (Docker/Kubernetes). Which of the following represents the GREATEST audit concern?',
 '["Containers share the host operating system kernel", "Container images are immutable once deployed", "Kubernetes provides automated scaling and self-healing", "Container orchestration enables declarative configuration management"]'::jsonb,
 0,
 'Containers sharing the host OS kernel is the greatest audit concern because a kernel vulnerability could potentially affect all containers on that host. Unlike virtual machines, which have separate OS instances, containers rely on kernel-level isolation. If the shared kernel is compromised, all containers may be exposed. Immutability, auto-scaling, and declarative configs are generally positive security and operational features.',
 'hard'),

-- ============================================================
-- ERP and Accounting Information Systems (14 questions)
-- ============================================================

(5, 'ERP and Accounting Information Systems',
 'Which of the following best describes an Enterprise Resource Planning (ERP) system?',
 '["A standalone accounting application for general ledger management", "An integrated suite of modules that shares a common database across business functions", "A spreadsheet-based system for financial consolidation", "A point-of-sale system for retail transactions"]'::jsonb,
 1,
 'An ERP system integrates multiple business functions — finance, HR, supply chain, manufacturing, sales — into a single system with a shared database. This integration eliminates data silos, ensures consistency, and provides real-time visibility across the organization. Standalone applications, spreadsheets, and POS systems lack this cross-functional integration.',
 'easy'),

(5, 'ERP and Accounting Information Systems',
 'During an ERP implementation, the project team must decide whether to accept the system''s built-in processes or modify them. What is the primary risk of excessive customization?',
 '["The system will run faster due to optimized code", "Future upgrades and patches may break customized functionality", "The implementation timeline will be shortened", "The vendor will provide enhanced support for customized modules"]'::jsonb,
 1,
 'Excessive customization creates technical debt that makes future upgrades difficult and costly because vendor patches and updates may conflict with custom code. Organizations must regression-test all customizations with each upgrade, and the vendor typically does not support custom modifications.',
 'medium'),

(5, 'ERP and Accounting Information Systems',
 'In an accounting information system, which type of control ensures that all authorized transactions are recorded and that no unauthorized transactions are processed?',
 '["Output controls", "Input controls", "Processing controls", "Storage controls"]'::jsonb,
 1,
 'Input controls operate at the point of data entry to ensure completeness (all authorized transactions are recorded), accuracy (data is correct), and authorization (only valid transactions are processed). These include validation checks, batch totals, sequence checks, and authorization requirements. Processing, output, and storage controls operate at later stages.',
 'easy'),

(5, 'ERP and Accounting Information Systems',
 'A company processes 10,000 invoices daily through its ERP system. To ensure completeness, which batch control is most effective?',
 '["Hash total of invoice numbers", "Visual inspection of printed reports", "Random sampling of 50 invoices", "Record count and financial total of the batch"]'::jsonb,
 3,
 'A record count (verifying 10,000 records were processed) combined with a financial total (verifying the dollar total matches the control total established before processing) provides the most effective assurance of both completeness and accuracy. Hash totals help but don''t verify dollar accuracy. Visual inspection and sampling provide limited assurance for large volumes.',
 'medium'),

(5, 'ERP and Accounting Information Systems',
 'Which of the following describes a master file in an accounting information system?',
 '["A temporary file used to store transactions before posting", "A permanent file containing cumulative data about entities such as customers, vendors, or employees", "A log file that records system errors and exceptions", "A report file generated at period-end for management review"]'::jsonb,
 1,
 'A master file is a permanent repository of relatively static data about entities — customer names and addresses, vendor payment terms, employee pay rates, and chart of accounts. It is updated by transaction processing but persists across accounting periods. Transaction files are temporary, log files record events, and report files are generated outputs.',
 'easy'),

(5, 'ERP and Accounting Information Systems',
 'An auditor is testing automated controls in an ERP system. The system is configured to reject purchase orders exceeding $50,000 without a VP-level approval. This is an example of:',
 '["A detective control", "A corrective control", "A preventive control", "A compensating control"]'::jsonb,
 2,
 'Rejecting transactions that don''t meet authorization requirements before they are processed is a preventive control — it prevents unauthorized transactions from entering the system. Detective controls identify issues after processing, corrective controls fix errors after detection, and compensating controls substitute for other controls that are not feasible.',
 'easy'),

(5, 'ERP and Accounting Information Systems',
 'An organization discovers that journal entries posted through the ERP system sometimes bypass the standard approval workflow due to a configuration error. Which of the following is the MOST appropriate immediate action?',
 '["Implement a detective control to review all journal entries posted in the past month", "Correct the configuration error and re-test the approval workflow", "Switch to a manual journal entry approval process", "Disable journal entry posting until the next system upgrade"]'::jsonb,
 1,
 'The most appropriate immediate action is to fix the root cause (the configuration error) and verify the fix through testing. While reviewing past entries (detective) is also necessary, it addresses the symptom, not the cause. Switching to manual processes or disabling posting creates operational disruption disproportionate to the issue.',
 'medium'),

(5, 'ERP and Accounting Information Systems',
 'Which of the following audit procedures is MOST effective for testing the accuracy of automated calculations in an ERP system?',
 '["Interviewing the IT manager about system capabilities", "Reviewing the system user manual", "Performing a parallel simulation using independent software to recalculate results", "Inspecting the system configuration screen"]'::jsonb,
 2,
 'Parallel simulation involves using independent software (such as CAAT tools or spreadsheets) to reprocess actual transaction data and compare results with the ERP''s output. This directly tests whether the automated calculations produce accurate results. Interviews, manuals, and configuration screens provide evidence about design but not operating effectiveness.',
 'medium'),

(5, 'ERP and Accounting Information Systems',
 'A company''s ERP system uses three-way matching for accounts payable. Which three documents are compared?',
 '["Purchase order, receiving report, and vendor invoice", "Purchase requisition, purchase order, and check", "Vendor invoice, bank statement, and general ledger", "Sales order, shipping document, and customer payment"]'::jsonb,
 0,
 'Three-way matching compares the purchase order (what was ordered), the receiving report (what was received), and the vendor invoice (what the vendor is billing). Discrepancies among these three documents trigger an exception for investigation before payment is authorized. This control prevents payment for goods not ordered or not received.',
 'medium'),

(5, 'ERP and Accounting Information Systems',
 'During an audit of an ERP system, the auditor identifies that a single user has the ability to create vendors, approve invoices, and process payments. This is a violation of:',
 '["Least privilege principle", "Segregation of duties", "Defense in depth", "Change management policy"]'::jsonb,
 1,
 'Segregation of duties (SoD) requires that no single individual should control all phases of a transaction — authorization, custody, and recording. Having one user who can create vendors, approve invoices, and process payments creates the risk of fictitious vendor fraud. While least privilege is related, the core issue is the lack of separation between incompatible functions.',
 'medium'),

(5, 'ERP and Accounting Information Systems',
 'A manufacturing company is evaluating ERP modules. Which module would MOST directly support accurate Work-in-Process (WIP) inventory valuation for financial reporting?',
 '["Customer Relationship Management (CRM)", "Manufacturing Resource Planning (MRP)", "Human Capital Management (HCM)", "Procurement and Sourcing"]'::jsonb,
 1,
 'The MRP module tracks bills of materials, production orders, labor, and overhead allocation — all essential for calculating WIP inventory values at each production stage. CRM focuses on customer interactions, HCM on workforce management, and procurement on purchasing — none directly compute WIP valuation.',
 'hard'),

(5, 'ERP and Accounting Information Systems',
 'An organization migrates from five legacy accounting systems to a single ERP platform. During data migration, the team discovers inconsistent chart of accounts structures across the legacy systems. What is the BEST approach?',
 '["Import all legacy data as-is and reconcile differences after go-live", "Create a unified chart of accounts mapping and transform legacy data during migration", "Keep the five legacy systems running alongside the ERP indefinitely", "Discard historical data and start fresh in the new ERP"]'::jsonb,
 1,
 'Creating a unified chart of accounts mapping before migration ensures data consistency and comparability in the new system. Importing inconsistent data creates reporting problems, parallel indefinite operation defeats the purpose of consolidation, and discarding history violates retention requirements and eliminates comparative data.',
 'hard'),

(5, 'ERP and Accounting Information Systems',
 'Which of the following ERP system configurations would BEST support compliance with the Sarbanes-Oxley Act (SOX) Section 404 requirements?',
 '["Disabling audit trails to improve system performance", "Implementing role-based access controls with documented SoD matrices", "Granting super-user access to all accounting staff for efficiency", "Allowing direct database modifications to correct posting errors"]'::jsonb,
 1,
 'SOX Section 404 requires management to assess and report on internal controls over financial reporting. Role-based access controls with documented SoD matrices provide a framework for demonstrating that access is appropriately restricted and incompatible duties are separated. Disabling audit trails, granting broad access, and allowing direct database changes all undermine internal controls.',
 'hard'),

(5, 'ERP and Accounting Information Systems',
 'An ERP system generates automated recurring journal entries for monthly depreciation. An auditor wants to test whether the depreciation rates are correctly configured. Which audit approach is MOST efficient?',
 '["Manually recalculate depreciation for every fixed asset in the register", "Review the ERP configuration settings for depreciation methods and rates against the accounting policy, then test a sample of assets", "Accept the automated output without testing because it is system-generated", "Test only new assets acquired in the current year"]'::jsonb,
 1,
 'The most efficient approach combines reviewing configuration (confirming the right methods and rates are set up per policy) with testing a sample of assets to verify the calculations. Testing every asset is impractical, accepting without testing provides no assurance, and testing only new assets ignores potential configuration changes affecting existing assets.',
 'hard'),

-- ============================================================
-- Data Management and Lifecycle (14 questions)
-- ============================================================

(5, 'Data Management and Lifecycle',
 'Which of the following best describes the concept of "data governance"?',
 '["The technical process of backing up databases nightly", "The framework of policies, roles, and standards that ensure data quality, security, and compliance throughout its lifecycle", "The physical security of data center facilities", "The process of encrypting all data at rest"]'::jsonb,
 1,
 'Data governance is the overall management of data availability, usability, integrity, and security within an organization. It encompasses policies, procedures, roles (data owners, stewards, custodians), and standards that ensure data is managed as a strategic asset throughout its lifecycle from creation to disposal.',
 'easy'),

(5, 'Data Management and Lifecycle',
 'An organization classifies its data into categories: Public, Internal, Confidential, and Restricted. Which classification should apply to employee Social Security numbers?',
 '["Public", "Internal", "Confidential", "Restricted"]'::jsonb,
 3,
 'Social Security numbers are highly sensitive personally identifiable information (PII) subject to regulatory protection. The Restricted classification — the highest sensitivity level — is appropriate because unauthorized disclosure could result in identity theft, regulatory penalties, and significant harm to individuals.',
 'easy'),

(5, 'Data Management and Lifecycle',
 'Which phase of the data lifecycle involves securely eliminating data that is no longer needed, in compliance with retention policies?',
 '["Data creation", "Data storage", "Data archival", "Data destruction"]'::jsonb,
 3,
 'Data destruction (also called data disposal or sanitization) is the final phase of the data lifecycle where data that has exceeded its retention period is securely eliminated using methods appropriate to the data''s classification — such as cryptographic erasure, degaussing, or physical destruction of media.',
 'easy'),

(5, 'Data Management and Lifecycle',
 'A company maintains its general ledger data in a relational database. Which database concept ensures that every transaction record references a valid account in the chart of accounts?',
 '["Primary key constraint", "Referential integrity (foreign key) constraint", "Check constraint", "Unique constraint"]'::jsonb,
 1,
 'Referential integrity enforced through foreign key constraints ensures that a value in one table (e.g., account_id in a transaction table) must match a valid value in the referenced table (e.g., the chart of accounts). This prevents orphaned records and maintains data consistency across related tables.',
 'medium'),

(5, 'Data Management and Lifecycle',
 'An auditor discovers that a client''s database allows null values in the "transaction_amount" field of the journal entry table. What data quality dimension is at risk?',
 '["Timeliness", "Completeness", "Uniqueness", "Consistency"]'::jsonb,
 1,
 'Completeness means that all required data elements are present. Allowing null values in a mandatory field like transaction_amount means records can exist without this critical information, undermining the completeness and reliability of financial data.',
 'medium'),

(5, 'Data Management and Lifecycle',
 'Which of the following data retention practices presents the GREATEST compliance risk for an organization?',
 '["Retaining tax records for seven years per IRS requirements", "Implementing automated deletion policies based on retention schedules", "Retaining all data indefinitely without a defined retention policy", "Archiving data to read-only storage after the active retention period"]'::jsonb,
 2,
 'Retaining all data indefinitely without a policy creates multiple risks: increased storage costs, greater exposure surface in data breaches, potential litigation holds on unnecessary data, and difficulty complying with privacy regulations (like GDPR) that require data minimization. A defined retention and disposal policy is essential for compliance.',
 'medium'),

(5, 'Data Management and Lifecycle',
 'A financial institution is required to produce an audit trail showing every modification to customer account records over the past five years. Which database feature BEST supports this requirement?',
 '["Database indexing", "Table partitioning", "Change data capture (CDC) or temporal tables", "Database normalization"]'::jsonb,
 2,
 'Change data capture (CDC) and temporal tables record every change made to database records, preserving the before and after values along with timestamps and the user who made the change. This creates a comprehensive audit trail. Indexing improves query speed, partitioning improves manageability, and normalization reduces redundancy — none create audit trails.',
 'medium'),

(5, 'Data Management and Lifecycle',
 'An organization needs to share financial data with a third-party analytics provider while protecting personally identifiable information. Which technique replaces sensitive fields with realistic but non-reversible substitutes?',
 '["Encryption", "Tokenization", "Data masking", "Data compression"]'::jsonb,
 2,
 'Data masking replaces sensitive data with realistic but fictitious values (e.g., replacing real names with generated names) in a non-reversible way, making it suitable for sharing with third parties. Encryption is reversible with a key, tokenization maps to a vault (reversible by design), and compression is not a data protection technique.',
 'medium'),

(5, 'Data Management and Lifecycle',
 'Which of the following describes the difference between a data owner and a data custodian?',
 '["The data owner is responsible for daily backups; the custodian sets classification levels", "The data owner sets policies and classification; the custodian implements technical controls and daily management", "The data owner and custodian have identical responsibilities", "The data custodian has more authority than the data owner"]'::jsonb,
 1,
 'The data owner (typically a business manager) is accountable for data classification, access authorization policies, and ensuring data quality. The data custodian (typically IT) is responsible for implementing the owner''s directives through technical controls — backups, access implementation, encryption, and day-to-day data management.',
 'medium'),

(5, 'Data Management and Lifecycle',
 'A company performs an Extract, Transform, Load (ETL) process to populate its financial data warehouse. During testing, the auditor discovers that 2% of source records were dropped during transformation. What should the auditor recommend?',
 '["Accept the 2% variance as immaterial", "Implement reconciliation controls that compare record counts and financial totals between source and target after each ETL run", "Replace the ETL process with manual data entry", "Reduce the frequency of ETL runs to weekly"]'::jsonb,
 1,
 'Reconciliation controls that compare record counts and financial totals between source and target systems after each ETL run ensure completeness and accuracy of data transfers. A 2% data loss could be material depending on which records were dropped. Manual entry is impractical, reducing frequency doesn''t fix the issue, and accepting without investigation is inappropriate.',
 'hard'),

(5, 'Data Management and Lifecycle',
 'Under GDPR, what is the maximum time an organization has to notify the supervisory authority after discovering a personal data breach?',
 '["24 hours", "48 hours", "72 hours", "7 calendar days"]'::jsonb,
 2,
 'Under GDPR Article 33, the data controller must notify the supervisory authority within 72 hours of becoming aware of a personal data breach, unless the breach is unlikely to result in a risk to individuals'' rights and freedoms. If notification is delayed beyond 72 hours, the controller must provide reasons for the delay.',
 'easy'),

(5, 'Data Management and Lifecycle',
 'An organization''s data warehouse contains seven years of transaction history across 50 million records. A regulatory audit requires querying specific date ranges efficiently. Which database strategy BEST addresses this requirement?',
 '["Full table scans with increased server RAM", "Table partitioning by date range (e.g., monthly or yearly partitions)", "Denormalization of all tables into a single flat file", "Converting the database to a NoSQL document store"]'::jsonb,
 1,
 'Table partitioning by date range enables the database engine to scan only the relevant partitions (partition pruning) rather than the entire 50-million-record table, dramatically improving query performance for date-range-specific regulatory queries. Full table scans are inefficient, denormalization into flat files loses relational integrity, and NoSQL is not inherently better for this structured use case.',
 'hard'),

(5, 'Data Management and Lifecycle',
 'Which of the following techniques provides the STRONGEST assurance that data has not been tampered with during transmission?',
 '["Parity check", "Checksum", "Cryptographic hash (e.g., SHA-256) with digital signature", "Cyclic redundancy check (CRC)"]'::jsonb,
 2,
 'A cryptographic hash combined with a digital signature provides the strongest tamper-detection assurance. The hash creates a unique fingerprint of the data, and the digital signature authenticates the sender and ensures the hash itself hasn''t been altered. Parity checks, checksums, and CRCs detect accidental errors but are not resistant to deliberate tampering.',
 'hard'),

(5, 'Data Management and Lifecycle',
 'A healthcare organization must comply with HIPAA data retention requirements for patient billing records while minimizing storage costs. Which approach BEST balances compliance with cost efficiency?',
 '["Delete all records older than one year to save storage costs", "Store active records on high-performance storage and archive older records to lower-cost tiered storage with appropriate access controls", "Print all records and store paper copies in a warehouse", "Keep all records on the primary production database indefinitely"]'::jsonb,
 1,
 'Tiered storage moves older data to less expensive storage media (e.g., cold storage, tape) while maintaining accessibility for compliance requirements. This balances the regulatory requirement to retain records (HIPAA requires six years minimum for billing records) with cost efficiency. Deleting early violates retention rules, paper storage is impractical, and keeping everything on production storage is unnecessarily expensive.',
 'hard'),

-- ============================================================
-- System Availability and Change Management (14 questions)
-- ============================================================

(5, 'System Availability and Change Management',
 'Which of the following metrics measures the total amount of acceptable data loss during a system outage?',
 '["Recovery Time Objective (RTO)", "Recovery Point Objective (RPO)", "Mean Time Between Failures (MTBF)", "Mean Time To Repair (MTTR)"]'::jsonb,
 1,
 'Recovery Point Objective (RPO) defines the maximum acceptable amount of data loss measured in time — for example, an RPO of 1 hour means the organization can tolerate losing up to 1 hour of data. RTO measures how quickly systems must be restored, MTBF measures reliability between failures, and MTTR measures how long repairs take.',
 'easy'),

(5, 'System Availability and Change Management',
 'A company''s financial system has an RTO of 2 hours and an RPO of 15 minutes. Which backup strategy BEST meets the RPO requirement?',
 '["Daily full backups performed at midnight", "Hourly incremental backups", "Continuous data replication with transaction log shipping every 10 minutes", "Weekly full backups with daily differential backups"]'::jsonb,
 2,
 'An RPO of 15 minutes means no more than 15 minutes of data can be lost. Transaction log shipping every 10 minutes (within the 15-minute window) combined with continuous data replication ensures the most recent data is preserved within the RPO threshold. Daily, hourly, and weekly backups all exceed the 15-minute RPO.',
 'medium'),

(5, 'System Availability and Change Management',
 'Which of the following is the FIRST step in a formal change management process?',
 '["Deploying the change to production", "Submitting a change request with business justification", "Testing the change in a development environment", "Rolling back the change if it fails"]'::jsonb,
 1,
 'A formal change management process begins with submitting a change request that documents the proposed change, its business justification, risk assessment, and implementation plan. This request is reviewed and approved before development, testing, and deployment occur. Rollback procedures are prepared as part of the plan but executed only if deployment fails.',
 'easy'),

(5, 'System Availability and Change Management',
 'An organization''s change advisory board (CAB) reviews a proposed modification to the ERP system''s tax calculation module before year-end. Which of the following is the MOST important consideration for the CAB?',
 '["Whether the change will improve the user interface appearance", "The potential impact on financial reporting accuracy and the availability of rollback procedures", "Whether the change was requested by a senior executive", "The programming language used to implement the change"]'::jsonb,
 1,
 'The CAB should prioritize the change''s potential impact on financial reporting accuracy — especially for a tax calculation module near year-end — and ensure reliable rollback procedures exist if the change causes issues. UI appearance, requester seniority, and programming language are secondary to financial reporting integrity.',
 'medium'),

(5, 'System Availability and Change Management',
 'During an IT audit, the auditor discovers that emergency changes to the production financial system are implemented without prior CAB approval but are documented and reviewed within 48 hours. Is this practice acceptable?',
 '["No — all changes must receive prior CAB approval without exception", "Yes — emergency change procedures that include post-implementation review are an accepted practice in ITIL and COBIT frameworks", "No — emergency changes should never be made to financial systems", "Yes — but only if the changes are made by the CIO personally"]'::jsonb,
 1,
 'Industry frameworks (ITIL, COBIT) recognize that emergency changes may be necessary when system outages or critical defects affect operations. The key control is that emergency changes follow an abbreviated but documented process and receive post-implementation review within a defined timeframe. Complete prohibition of emergency changes is impractical; requiring CIO involvement is not a recognized control.',
 'medium'),

(5, 'System Availability and Change Management',
 'A company''s business continuity plan defines three tiers of systems. Tier 1 systems must be recovered within 1 hour. Which of the following financial systems should MOST likely be classified as Tier 1?',
 '["The employee expense reporting portal", "The real-time payment processing and general ledger system", "The annual budgeting application", "The historical financial report archive"]'::jsonb,
 1,
 'Real-time payment processing and the general ledger are critical financial operations that directly affect the organization''s ability to conduct business. Downtime in these systems can cause failed payments, regulatory non-compliance, and financial losses. Expense reporting, annual budgeting, and historical archives are important but can tolerate longer recovery times.',
 'medium'),

(5, 'System Availability and Change Management',
 'Which of the following testing types validates that a software change has not adversely affected existing functionality?',
 '["Unit testing", "Integration testing", "Regression testing", "User acceptance testing"]'::jsonb,
 2,
 'Regression testing specifically validates that new changes, bug fixes, or enhancements have not introduced unintended side effects in existing functionality. Unit testing verifies individual components, integration testing verifies component interactions, and user acceptance testing validates that the system meets business requirements.',
 'easy'),

(5, 'System Availability and Change Management',
 'An auditor reviews the change management log and notes that 15% of production changes were classified as "emergency" in the past quarter. What should the auditor conclude?',
 '["This is normal and requires no further investigation", "The high percentage suggests that the change management process may be circumvented by classifying routine changes as emergencies to avoid CAB review", "Emergency changes should be eliminated entirely", "The auditor should only review non-emergency changes"]'::jsonb,
 1,
 'Industry benchmarks typically show emergency changes at 5-10% of total changes. A 15% rate suggests potential process circumvention, where staff may classify routine changes as emergencies to bypass the standard approval process. The auditor should investigate whether these changes truly met emergency criteria and recommend strengthening the emergency classification criteria.',
 'hard'),

(5, 'System Availability and Change Management',
 'A financial services company conducts annual disaster recovery testing by performing a full failover to its secondary data center. Which type of DR test is this?',
 '["Tabletop exercise", "Walkthrough test", "Simulation test", "Full interruption test"]'::jsonb,
 3,
 'A full interruption test (also called a full-scale test) involves actually switching operations from the primary site to the backup site, testing all recovery procedures under real conditions. A tabletop exercise discusses procedures, a walkthrough reviews steps without executing them, and a simulation tests specific scenarios without actual failover.',
 'easy'),

(5, 'System Availability and Change Management',
 'A company has two data centers 500 miles apart. The primary site handles all financial processing, and the secondary site receives asynchronous data replication with a 30-minute lag. A disaster destroys the primary site at 2:00 PM. What is the maximum data loss?',
 '["Zero — no data is lost because of replication", "Up to 30 minutes of transactions (those processed after the last successful replication)", "Exactly 30 minutes of data", "All data since the last daily backup"]'::jsonb,
 1,
 'With asynchronous replication running on a 30-minute lag, the secondary site contains data up to 30 minutes behind the primary. If the disaster occurs at 2:00 PM and the last successful replication completed at 1:30 PM, up to 30 minutes of transactions could be lost. The actual loss depends on when the last replication cycle completed.',
 'medium'),

(5, 'System Availability and Change Management',
 'Which of the following BEST describes the purpose of a configuration management database (CMDB)?',
 '["To store user passwords securely", "To maintain a comprehensive record of IT assets, their configurations, and relationships", "To track employee attendance", "To process financial transactions"]'::jsonb,
 1,
 'A CMDB maintains a comprehensive inventory of IT assets (configuration items), their attributes (versions, configurations, owners), and the relationships between them. This information supports change management, incident management, and audit activities by providing visibility into the IT environment.',
 'medium'),

(5, 'System Availability and Change Management',
 'An organization calculates that its financial transaction system has an MTBF of 2,000 hours and an MTTR of 4 hours. What is the system''s availability percentage?',
 '["99.0%", "99.5%", "99.8%", "99.9%"]'::jsonb,
 2,
 'Availability = MTBF / (MTBF + MTTR) = 2,000 / (2,000 + 4) = 2,000 / 2,004 = 0.998 or 99.8%. This calculation shows the proportion of time the system is operational versus total time (operational plus repair time).',
 'hard'),

(5, 'System Availability and Change Management',
 'A company is planning a major ERP upgrade that will change the general ledger posting logic. The change management process requires parallel testing. What does this involve?',
 '["Running the old and new systems simultaneously on the same transactions and comparing results", "Testing the new system in isolation without reference to the old system", "Deploying the change to half of the user base while the other half uses the old system", "Performing the upgrade during non-business hours to minimize risk"]'::jsonb,
 0,
 'Parallel testing involves running both the old and new systems simultaneously, processing the same transactions through both, and comparing outputs to verify the new system produces correct results. This provides high assurance that the new system works correctly before the old system is decommissioned.',
 'medium'),

(5, 'System Availability and Change Management',
 'An IT auditor discovers that a developer has direct write access to the production database of the financial system. No separation exists between development and production environments. Which COBIT principle is most directly violated?',
 '["Stakeholder Value Delivery", "Holistic Approach", "Separation of Environments and Duties", "Continuous Improvement"]'::jsonb,
 2,
 'The principle of separation of environments and duties requires that development, testing, and production environments be separated and that developers should not have the ability to modify production systems directly. This prevents unauthorized or untested changes from affecting live financial data and supports the integrity of financial reporting.',
 'hard'),

-- ============================================================
-- Security and Control Frameworks (14 questions)
-- ============================================================

(5, 'Security and Control Frameworks',
 'Which framework was specifically designed to help organizations manage and reduce cybersecurity risk and is published by the National Institute of Standards and Technology?',
 '["COBIT 2019", "ISO 27001", "NIST Cybersecurity Framework (CSF)", "ITIL v4"]'::jsonb,
 2,
 'The NIST Cybersecurity Framework (CSF) was developed by NIST to provide a voluntary framework for managing and reducing cybersecurity risk. It consists of five core functions: Identify, Protect, Detect, Respond, and Recover. COBIT focuses on IT governance, ISO 27001 on information security management systems, and ITIL on IT service management.',
 'easy'),

(5, 'Security and Control Frameworks',
 'Under the NIST Cybersecurity Framework, which core function includes activities related to developing and implementing safeguards to ensure delivery of critical services?',
 '["Identify", "Protect", "Detect", "Respond"]'::jsonb,
 1,
 'The Protect function includes access control, awareness and training, data security, information protection processes, maintenance, and protective technology. It focuses on implementing appropriate safeguards to limit or contain the impact of potential cybersecurity events.',
 'easy'),

(5, 'Security and Control Frameworks',
 'COBIT 2019 is built on which of the following principles?',
 '["Provide Stakeholder Value, Holistic Approach, Dynamic Governance System", "Confidentiality, Integrity, Availability", "Plan, Do, Check, Act", "Identify, Protect, Detect, Respond, Recover"]'::jsonb,
 0,
 'COBIT 2019 is built on six principles: Provide Stakeholder Value, Holistic Approach, Dynamic Governance System, Governance Distinct from Management, Tailored to Enterprise Needs, and End-to-End Governance System. CIA is an information security triad, PDCA is a quality management cycle, and the five functions belong to the NIST CSF.',
 'medium'),

(5, 'Security and Control Frameworks',
 'An organization wants to achieve ISO 27001 certification. Which of the following is a mandatory requirement?',
 '["Implementing all 93 controls listed in Annex A", "Establishing an Information Security Management System (ISMS) with a risk-based approach", "Using the NIST CSF as the basis for the ISMS", "Hiring a Chief Information Security Officer (CISO)"]'::jsonb,
 1,
 'ISO 27001 mandates establishing, implementing, maintaining, and continually improving an ISMS using a risk-based approach. Not all 93 Annex A controls must be implemented — organizations select controls based on their risk assessment and document unselected controls in a Statement of Applicability. Using NIST or hiring a CISO are not ISO 27001 requirements.',
 'medium'),

(5, 'Security and Control Frameworks',
 'Which of the following BEST describes the relationship between COBIT and NIST CSF?',
 '["They are competing frameworks that cannot be used together", "COBIT focuses on IT governance while NIST CSF focuses on cybersecurity risk management; they are complementary", "NIST CSF is a subset of COBIT", "COBIT has replaced NIST CSF as the industry standard"]'::jsonb,
 1,
 'COBIT and NIST CSF are complementary frameworks addressing different aspects of IT management. COBIT provides a comprehensive IT governance and management framework covering the full IT function, while NIST CSF specifically addresses cybersecurity risk management. Organizations often use both: COBIT for governance and NIST CSF for cybersecurity.',
 'medium'),

(5, 'Security and Control Frameworks',
 'The COSO Internal Control — Integrated Framework identifies five components of internal control. Which component is considered the foundation for all others?',
 '["Risk Assessment", "Control Activities", "Control Environment", "Monitoring Activities"]'::jsonb,
 2,
 'The Control Environment is the foundation of the COSO framework and sets the tone of an organization. It includes governance structure, ethical values, management philosophy, organizational structure, competence standards, and accountability. The other four components — Risk Assessment, Control Activities, Information and Communication, and Monitoring — operate within the context established by the control environment.',
 'easy'),

(5, 'Security and Control Frameworks',
 'An IT auditor is evaluating whether a client''s information security controls are aligned with a recognized framework. The client demonstrates that controls map to ISO 27001 domains but has not performed a formal risk assessment. What should the auditor conclude?',
 '["The controls are adequate because they map to a recognized framework", "The controls may be misaligned because ISO 27001 requires controls to be selected based on risk assessment results", "Risk assessment is optional under ISO 27001", "The auditor should recommend switching to NIST CSF instead"]'::jsonb,
 1,
 'ISO 27001 requires a risk-based approach where controls are selected based on the results of a formal risk assessment. Simply mapping controls to Annex A without conducting a risk assessment means the organization may have implemented unnecessary controls while missing critical ones specific to their risk profile.',
 'medium'),

(5, 'Security and Control Frameworks',
 'Which of the following represents the correct hierarchy from broadest to most specific in IT governance?',
 '["Procedures → Policies → Standards → Guidelines", "Policies → Standards → Procedures → Guidelines", "Guidelines → Standards → Policies → Procedures", "Standards → Policies → Guidelines → Procedures"]'::jsonb,
 1,
 'The governance hierarchy flows from broadest to most specific: Policies (high-level statements of intent), Standards (mandatory requirements supporting policies), Procedures (step-by-step instructions for implementing standards), and Guidelines (recommended practices). Policies set direction, standards define requirements, procedures explain how, and guidelines suggest best practices.',
 'medium'),

(5, 'Security and Control Frameworks',
 'A company uses COBIT 2019 to assess its IT governance maturity. The company has documented processes and some metrics but inconsistent implementation across departments. At which COBIT capability level does this BEST fall?',
 '["Level 1 — Performed", "Level 2 — Managed", "Level 3 — Established", "Level 4 — Predictable"]'::jsonb,
 1,
 'COBIT Level 2 (Managed) indicates that processes are planned, monitored, and adjusted, with work products appropriately managed. Having documented processes with some metrics but inconsistent implementation suggests the organization has moved beyond merely performing (Level 1) but has not yet achieved the consistent enterprise-wide implementation characteristic of Level 3 (Established).',
 'hard'),

(5, 'Security and Control Frameworks',
 'Which of the following is a key difference between ISO 27001 and ISO 27002?',
 '["ISO 27001 is certifiable; ISO 27002 provides implementation guidance for controls", "ISO 27002 is certifiable; ISO 27001 provides guidance only", "They are identical documents published by different organizations", "ISO 27001 covers cybersecurity; ISO 27002 covers physical security"]'::jsonb,
 0,
 'ISO 27001 specifies the requirements for establishing, implementing, and maintaining an ISMS and is the certifiable standard. ISO 27002 provides detailed implementation guidance and best practices for the controls listed in ISO 27001 Annex A. Organizations are certified against 27001, using 27002 as a reference for implementing controls.',
 'hard'),

(5, 'Security and Control Frameworks',
 'The NIST Risk Management Framework (RMF) consists of seven steps. Which step involves determining whether the security and privacy controls are implemented correctly and producing desired outcomes?',
 '["Categorize", "Select", "Implement", "Assess"]'::jsonb,
 3,
 'The Assess step of the NIST RMF evaluates whether security and privacy controls are implemented correctly, operating as intended, and producing the desired outcome with respect to meeting the security and privacy requirements. The seven steps are: Prepare, Categorize, Select, Implement, Assess, Authorize, and Monitor.',
 'hard'),

(5, 'Security and Control Frameworks',
 'An organization''s board of directors wants to ensure that IT strategy aligns with business objectives. According to COBIT 2019, which governance objective MOST directly addresses this need?',
 '["EDM01: Ensured Governance Framework Setting and Maintenance", "EDM02: Ensured Benefits Delivery", "APO02: Managed Strategy", "BAI01: Managed Programs"]'::jsonb,
 2,
 'APO02 (Managed Strategy) in COBIT 2019 directly addresses aligning IT strategy with business objectives by defining how IT can best contribute to business goals. While EDM01 establishes the governance framework and EDM02 ensures benefits delivery, APO02 specifically handles the strategic alignment between IT and business.',
 'hard'),

(5, 'Security and Control Frameworks',
 'Which of the following control types is designed to discourage individuals from committing policy violations?',
 '["Detective control", "Corrective control", "Deterrent control", "Compensating control"]'::jsonb,
 2,
 'Deterrent controls discourage individuals from intentionally violating policies or procedures. Examples include warning banners, surveillance cameras, and disciplinary policies. Detective controls identify violations after they occur, corrective controls fix problems, and compensating controls substitute for primary controls.',
 'easy'),

(5, 'Security and Control Frameworks',
 'An organization implements a defense-in-depth strategy for its financial systems. Which of the following BEST represents this approach?',
 '["Installing a single, comprehensive firewall at the network perimeter", "Implementing multiple layers of security controls: firewalls, IDS/IPS, access controls, encryption, and monitoring", "Relying exclusively on strong passwords for all systems", "Outsourcing all security responsibilities to a managed security provider"]'::jsonb,
 1,
 'Defense in depth employs multiple, overlapping layers of security controls so that if one layer fails, others continue to provide protection. This includes network controls (firewalls, IDS/IPS), host controls (endpoint protection, patching), application controls (input validation, authentication), data controls (encryption), and monitoring (SIEM, logging).',
 'medium'),

-- ============================================================
-- Threats, Attacks, and Vulnerabilities (14 questions)
-- ============================================================

(5, 'Threats, Attacks, and Vulnerabilities',
 'Which type of attack involves an attacker sending fraudulent emails that appear to come from a trusted source to trick recipients into revealing sensitive information?',
 '["Denial of Service (DoS)", "Phishing", "SQL injection", "Man-in-the-middle"]'::jsonb,
 1,
 'Phishing is a social engineering attack that uses fraudulent communications (typically email) appearing to come from a reputable source to trick individuals into revealing sensitive information such as credentials, financial data, or PII. DoS disrupts availability, SQL injection targets databases, and man-in-the-middle intercepts communications.',
 'easy'),

(5, 'Threats, Attacks, and Vulnerabilities',
 'An attacker inserts malicious SQL code into a web application''s login form, gaining unauthorized access to the underlying database. This is an example of:',
 '["Cross-site scripting (XSS)", "SQL injection", "Buffer overflow", "Session hijacking"]'::jsonb,
 1,
 'SQL injection occurs when an attacker inserts malicious SQL statements into input fields that are passed to the database for execution. If the application does not properly validate and sanitize input, the attacker can read, modify, or delete data, bypass authentication, or execute system commands. XSS injects client-side scripts, buffer overflow exceeds memory buffers, and session hijacking takes over user sessions.',
 'easy'),

(5, 'Threats, Attacks, and Vulnerabilities',
 'Which of the following threats is MOST difficult to prevent with technical controls alone?',
 '["Malware infection through unpatched software", "Social engineering attack targeting the CFO", "Brute force password attack", "Denial of service attack"]'::jsonb,
 1,
 'Social engineering exploits human psychology rather than technical vulnerabilities, making it difficult to prevent with technical controls alone. While email filters, URL reputation, and MFA help, the primary defense is security awareness training. Malware, brute force, and DoS attacks can be significantly mitigated through patching, account lockout, and network controls respectively.',
 'medium'),

(5, 'Threats, Attacks, and Vulnerabilities',
 'A company''s accounts payable clerk receives an email that appears to be from the CEO requesting an immediate wire transfer to a new vendor. The email address is slightly different from the CEO''s actual address. This is an example of:',
 '["Spear phishing / Business Email Compromise (BEC)", "Ransomware", "Zero-day exploit", "Brute force attack"]'::jsonb,
 0,
 'This describes a Business Email Compromise (BEC), a form of spear phishing targeting specific individuals with authority over financial transactions. The attacker impersonates a senior executive using a look-alike email domain to create urgency and bypass normal approval processes. BEC has resulted in billions of dollars in losses globally.',
 'medium'),

(5, 'Threats, Attacks, and Vulnerabilities',
 'Which type of malware encrypts a victim''s files and demands payment for the decryption key?',
 '["Spyware", "Adware", "Ransomware", "Rootkit"]'::jsonb,
 2,
 'Ransomware encrypts files on the victim''s system and demands payment (typically cryptocurrency) for the decryption key. It can spread through phishing emails, compromised websites, or unpatched vulnerabilities. Spyware secretly monitors user activity, adware displays unwanted advertisements, and rootkits provide persistent hidden access.',
 'easy'),

(5, 'Threats, Attacks, and Vulnerabilities',
 'An organization discovers that an attacker has been present in their network for six months, slowly exfiltrating financial data without triggering alerts. This type of attack is known as:',
 '["Denial of Service (DoS)", "Advanced Persistent Threat (APT)", "Drive-by download", "Script kiddie attack"]'::jsonb,
 1,
 'An Advanced Persistent Threat (APT) is a prolonged, targeted cyberattack in which an attacker gains unauthorized access to a network and remains undetected for an extended period. APTs are typically sophisticated, well-resourced, and focused on stealing valuable data rather than causing immediate disruption.',
 'medium'),

(5, 'Threats, Attacks, and Vulnerabilities',
 'Which of the following vulnerabilities would allow an attacker to intercept and modify data transmitted between a user''s browser and a financial web application?',
 '["Unencrypted HTTP communication (lack of TLS/SSL)", "Strong password policy", "Multi-factor authentication", "Encrypted VPN tunnel"]'::jsonb,
 0,
 'Unencrypted HTTP communication transmits data in plaintext, allowing an attacker positioned between the client and server (man-in-the-middle) to intercept, read, and modify the data in transit. TLS/SSL encryption (HTTPS) prevents this by encrypting the communication channel. Strong passwords, MFA, and VPNs address other security concerns.',
 'medium'),

(5, 'Threats, Attacks, and Vulnerabilities',
 'A vulnerability scanner identifies that a web server is running an outdated version of Apache with a known CVE that allows remote code execution. What is the MOST appropriate immediate action?',
 '["Ignore the finding because the server is behind a firewall", "Apply the security patch or upgrade to a supported version through the change management process", "Shut down the web server permanently", "Change the server''s IP address to obscure its identity"]'::jsonb,
 1,
 'The most appropriate action is to apply the security patch or upgrade to a supported version, following the organization''s change management process to ensure the fix doesn''t introduce other issues. Being behind a firewall doesn''t eliminate the vulnerability, shutting down disrupts operations, and changing IP addresses (security through obscurity) doesn''t fix the vulnerability.',
 'medium'),

(5, 'Threats, Attacks, and Vulnerabilities',
 'Which of the following BEST describes a zero-day vulnerability?',
 '["A vulnerability that was discovered and patched on the same day", "A vulnerability that is known to the attacker but not yet known to the vendor or public, with no patch available", "A vulnerability that only exists for 24 hours before being automatically patched", "A vulnerability that affects systems on the day they are first installed"]'::jsonb,
 1,
 'A zero-day vulnerability is a software flaw that is known to attackers but unknown to the software vendor, meaning no patch or fix exists yet. The term "zero-day" refers to the vendor having zero days to fix the issue before it can be exploited. These are particularly dangerous because traditional patch management cannot address them.',
 'easy'),

(5, 'Threats, Attacks, and Vulnerabilities',
 'An IT auditor discovers that a client''s web application is vulnerable to cross-site scripting (XSS). What is the PRIMARY risk of this vulnerability?',
 '["The database will be corrupted", "An attacker can inject malicious scripts that execute in other users'' browsers, potentially stealing session cookies or credentials", "The web server will experience a denial of service", "The application''s source code will be exposed"]'::jsonb,
 1,
 'XSS allows an attacker to inject malicious scripts into web pages viewed by other users. When the script executes in a victim''s browser, it can steal session cookies, capture keystrokes, redirect users to malicious sites, or modify page content. The attack targets the application''s users rather than the server itself.',
 'hard'),

(5, 'Threats, Attacks, and Vulnerabilities',
 'An organization''s SIEM system detects 50,000 failed login attempts against its VPN gateway from 200 different IP addresses in a 30-minute period. This pattern is MOST consistent with:',
 '["A single user who forgot their password", "A distributed brute force or credential stuffing attack", "Normal authentication traffic during business hours", "A misconfigured load balancer generating retry attempts"]'::jsonb,
 1,
 'The pattern of 50,000 failed attempts from 200 different IP addresses in a short period is consistent with a distributed brute force or credential stuffing attack, where attackers use stolen credentials from other breaches across many IP addresses to evade rate-limiting controls. A single user or misconfiguration would not generate this distributed pattern.',
 'hard'),

(5, 'Threats, Attacks, and Vulnerabilities',
 'Which attack technique specifically targets the supply chain by compromising a trusted software vendor''s update mechanism to distribute malware to the vendor''s customers?',
 '["Watering hole attack", "Supply chain attack", "DNS poisoning", "ARP spoofing"]'::jsonb,
 1,
 'A supply chain attack compromises a trusted vendor''s software development or distribution process to inject malicious code into legitimate software updates. When customers install the compromised update, they unknowingly deploy the malware. The SolarWinds Orion attack (2020) is a prominent example. Watering hole attacks compromise websites, DNS poisoning redirects traffic, and ARP spoofing targets local networks.',
 'hard'),

(5, 'Threats, Attacks, and Vulnerabilities',
 'An auditor is assessing threat likelihood for a risk assessment. Which factor is MOST relevant when evaluating the likelihood of an insider threat?',
 '["The geographic location of the data center", "The organization''s employee access levels, monitoring capabilities, and history of policy violations", "The speed of the internet connection", "The brand of firewall hardware used"]'::jsonb,
 1,
 'Insider threat likelihood depends on factors directly related to employees and their access: the breadth of access privileges, the effectiveness of monitoring and detection capabilities, separation of duties controls, and the organization''s history of policy violations and employee grievances. Technical infrastructure characteristics like location, bandwidth, and hardware brands are less relevant to insider threats.',
 'medium'),

(5, 'Threats, Attacks, and Vulnerabilities',
 'A financial institution''s threat intelligence team identifies that a nation-state threat actor is actively targeting organizations in their sector using a specific vulnerability in their VPN appliance. The institution uses the affected VPN product. According to the NIST risk assessment methodology, which risk response is MOST appropriate?',
 '["Accept the risk because nation-state actors are difficult to defend against", "Transfer the risk by purchasing cyber insurance and taking no other action", "Mitigate the risk by applying the vendor patch immediately, implementing compensating controls, and enhancing monitoring for indicators of compromise", "Avoid the risk by disconnecting all VPN connections permanently"]'::jsonb,
 2,
 'Given an active, targeted threat exploiting a specific vulnerability in a product the organization uses, immediate mitigation is the most appropriate response: patch the vulnerability, implement compensating controls (such as additional network segmentation), and enhance monitoring for indicators of compromise. Acceptance is inappropriate given the active threat, insurance alone doesn''t reduce the technical risk, and disconnecting VPN disrupts operations.',
 'hard'),

-- ============================================================
-- Security Controls and Monitoring (14 questions)
-- ============================================================

(5, 'Security Controls and Monitoring',
 'Which of the following authentication methods provides the STRONGEST security?',
 '["Username and password only", "Username, password, and a one-time code from an authenticator app", "Security question and answer", "Four-digit PIN"]'::jsonb,
 1,
 'Multi-factor authentication (MFA) combining something you know (password) with something you have (authenticator app generating a one-time code) provides significantly stronger security than single-factor methods. Security questions are easily researched, PINs have limited complexity, and passwords alone are vulnerable to phishing and credential stuffing.',
 'easy'),

(5, 'Security Controls and Monitoring',
 'An organization implements role-based access control (RBAC) for its ERP system. Which of the following BEST describes this approach?',
 '["Each user is granted individual permissions based on personal requests", "Users are assigned to roles, and roles are granted permissions based on job function", "All users receive the same level of access to ensure equality", "Access is granted based on the time of day the user logs in"]'::jsonb,
 1,
 'RBAC assigns users to roles that correspond to job functions, and permissions are granted to roles rather than individual users. This simplifies access management, supports segregation of duties, and ensures consistent access levels for users with the same job responsibilities. It is more manageable and auditable than individual permission assignment.',
 'easy'),

(5, 'Security Controls and Monitoring',
 'Which of the following is the PRIMARY purpose of a Security Information and Event Management (SIEM) system?',
 '["To prevent all cyberattacks from occurring", "To aggregate and correlate security event data from multiple sources to detect threats and support incident investigation", "To replace firewalls and intrusion detection systems", "To manage user passwords across the enterprise"]'::jsonb,
 1,
 'A SIEM system collects, aggregates, normalizes, and correlates log and event data from multiple sources (firewalls, IDS/IPS, servers, applications, endpoints) to detect security threats, trigger alerts, and support incident investigation and forensics. It does not prevent attacks, replace other security tools, or manage passwords.',
 'easy'),

(5, 'Security Controls and Monitoring',
 'An auditor reviews access control logs and discovers that a terminated employee''s account remained active for 45 days after their departure. Which control failure does this indicate?',
 '["Failure in the user provisioning process", "Failure in the user deprovisioning (offboarding) process", "Failure in the password complexity policy", "Failure in network segmentation"]'::jsonb,
 1,
 'This indicates a failure in the user deprovisioning (offboarding) process, which should ensure that access is revoked promptly when an employee is terminated. Active accounts for former employees create the risk of unauthorized access and are a common audit finding. Organizations should have automated or timely manual processes to disable accounts upon termination.',
 'medium'),

(5, 'Security Controls and Monitoring',
 'Which encryption approach protects data stored in a database (data at rest)?',
 '["TLS 1.3", "IPsec VPN", "Transparent Data Encryption (TDE)", "SSH tunneling"]'::jsonb,
 2,
 'Transparent Data Encryption (TDE) encrypts the database files at the storage level, protecting data at rest without requiring application changes. TLS 1.3 and SSH protect data in transit between systems, and IPsec VPN encrypts network traffic — none specifically address database storage encryption.',
 'medium'),

(5, 'Security Controls and Monitoring',
 'An organization wants to ensure that privileged administrators cannot perform sensitive actions without oversight. Which control is MOST effective?',
 '["Requiring administrators to use strong passwords", "Implementing a privileged access management (PAM) solution with session recording and just-in-time access", "Allowing administrators to approve their own access requests", "Providing administrators with shared credentials for accountability"]'::jsonb,
 1,
 'Privileged Access Management (PAM) solutions provide oversight of privileged accounts through features like session recording (creating an audit trail), just-in-time access (granting privileges only when needed and for limited durations), credential vaulting, and approval workflows. Strong passwords alone provide no oversight, self-approval defeats the purpose, and shared credentials eliminate individual accountability.',
 'medium'),

(5, 'Security Controls and Monitoring',
 'Which of the following network security devices examines packet contents against known attack signatures and can actively block malicious traffic?',
 '["Router", "Switch", "Intrusion Prevention System (IPS)", "Load balancer"]'::jsonb,
 2,
 'An Intrusion Prevention System (IPS) monitors network traffic, compares it against known attack signatures and anomalous behavior patterns, and can actively block or drop malicious packets in real-time. Routers direct traffic, switches forward frames within a network, and load balancers distribute traffic across servers — none perform deep packet inspection for threat prevention.',
 'medium'),

(5, 'Security Controls and Monitoring',
 'An organization''s firewall logs show that outbound traffic from an accounting workstation is connecting to an IP address in a country where the company has no business operations, at 3:00 AM on a weekend. What should the security team investigate?',
 '["Nothing — this is likely a routine software update", "Potential data exfiltration or command-and-control communication from a compromised workstation", "A scheduled backup to cloud storage", "Normal employee remote work activity"]'::jsonb,
 1,
 'Unusual outbound traffic from a business workstation to a foreign IP address during non-business hours is a strong indicator of potential compromise — either data exfiltration (stealing data) or command-and-control (C2) communication with an attacker''s server. This pattern warrants immediate investigation including network forensics, endpoint analysis, and log review.',
 'medium'),

(5, 'Security Controls and Monitoring',
 'Which of the following BEST describes the principle of least privilege?',
 '["Users should have administrative access to troubleshoot their own issues", "Users should be granted only the minimum access necessary to perform their job duties", "All users should have read access to all systems for transparency", "Managers should have full access to all systems their team uses"]'::jsonb,
 1,
 'The principle of least privilege dictates that users, applications, and processes should receive only the minimum permissions necessary to perform their required functions — no more, no less. This limits the potential damage from accidents, errors, or malicious actions by constraining what any single account can access or modify.',
 'easy'),

(5, 'Security Controls and Monitoring',
 'An organization implements Data Loss Prevention (DLP) software. Which of the following scenarios would DLP MOST likely detect and prevent?',
 '["An employee accessing a public website during lunch", "An employee emailing a spreadsheet containing 500 Social Security numbers to a personal Gmail account", "An employee printing a non-sensitive memo", "An employee downloading a software update from the vendor site"]'::jsonb,
 1,
 'DLP systems monitor data in motion, at rest, and in use for sensitive content patterns (such as SSN formats, credit card numbers, or classified keywords). Emailing a file containing 500 SSNs to an external personal email would trigger DLP policies designed to prevent unauthorized disclosure of PII. The other activities don''t involve sensitive data movement.',
 'medium'),

(5, 'Security Controls and Monitoring',
 'A company implements a Web Application Firewall (WAF) in front of its customer portal. Which of the following attacks is the WAF MOST specifically designed to mitigate?',
 '["Physical theft of a server", "SQL injection and cross-site scripting attacks targeting the web application", "Insider theft of backup tapes", "Social engineering phone calls to the help desk"]'::jsonb,
 1,
 'A WAF inspects HTTP/HTTPS traffic to and from a web application, filtering and blocking common web attacks such as SQL injection, XSS, CSRF, and other OWASP Top 10 vulnerabilities. It operates at the application layer (Layer 7) and understands web application protocols. Physical theft, tape theft, and social engineering are outside its scope.',
 'hard'),

(5, 'Security Controls and Monitoring',
 'An auditor is testing the effectiveness of an organization''s security monitoring. The auditor sends a simulated phishing email to 100 employees and finds that 35 clicked the malicious link and 5 reported it to the security team. What metric should the auditor highlight as the GREATEST concern?',
 '["The 35% click rate", "The 5% reporting rate", "The 65% non-click rate", "The email delivery rate"]'::jsonb,
 1,
 'While the 35% click rate is concerning, the 5% reporting rate is the greatest concern because it means the organization lacks a culture of security awareness and reporting. Even with a high click rate, rapid reporting enables the security team to respond quickly and contain the incident. Without reports, compromised accounts go undetected. Security awareness programs should emphasize both avoidance and reporting.',
 'hard'),

(5, 'Security Controls and Monitoring',
 'Which of the following log sources is MOST critical for an auditor investigating potential unauthorized journal entry modifications?',
 '["Web server access logs", "Network firewall logs", "Database audit trail logs capturing DML operations on the journal entry table", "DHCP server logs"]'::jsonb,
 2,
 'Database audit trail logs that capture Data Manipulation Language (DML) operations (INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE) on the journal entry table provide direct evidence of who modified what data, when, and how. Web server logs show page requests, firewall logs show network connections, and DHCP logs show IP assignments — none directly capture database record changes.',
 'hard'),

(5, 'Security Controls and Monitoring',
 'An organization conducts quarterly vulnerability scans and annual penetration tests. What is the KEY difference between these two activities?',
 '["Vulnerability scans are manual; penetration tests are automated", "Vulnerability scans identify known weaknesses; penetration tests actively exploit vulnerabilities to determine real-world impact", "Penetration tests are less thorough than vulnerability scans", "There is no meaningful difference between them"]'::jsonb,
 1,
 'Vulnerability scans are automated tools that identify known vulnerabilities by comparing system configurations and software versions against databases of known weaknesses. Penetration tests go further by having skilled testers actively attempt to exploit identified vulnerabilities, chain them together, and demonstrate real-world attack scenarios and their potential business impact.',
 'hard'),

-- ============================================================
-- Privacy Requirements and Data Protection (14 questions)
-- ============================================================

(5, 'Privacy Requirements and Data Protection',
 'Which of the following regulations establishes comprehensive data protection rights for individuals within the European Union?',
 '["HIPAA", "SOX", "GDPR", "PCI DSS"]'::jsonb,
 2,
 'The General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR) is the EU''s comprehensive data protection law that grants individuals rights over their personal data, including the right to access, rectification, erasure, portability, and the right to object to processing. HIPAA covers US healthcare data, SOX covers financial reporting controls, and PCI DSS covers payment card data.',
 'easy'),

(5, 'Privacy Requirements and Data Protection',
 'Under GDPR, which of the following is NOT a lawful basis for processing personal data?',
 '["Consent of the data subject", "Performance of a contract", "The data controller''s commercial interest in maximizing revenue", "Compliance with a legal obligation"]'::jsonb,
 2,
 'GDPR Article 6 defines six lawful bases for processing: consent, contract performance, legal obligation, vital interests, public task, and legitimate interests. Commercial revenue maximization is not a lawful basis. While "legitimate interests" exists as a basis, it requires a balancing test and cannot be used simply to maximize profit.',
 'medium'),

(5, 'Privacy Requirements and Data Protection',
 'The California Consumer Privacy Act (CCPA) grants consumers several rights. Which of the following is a right under CCPA?',
 '["The right to vote on company board elections", "The right to know what personal information is collected and to request its deletion", "The right to access the company''s financial statements", "The right to modify the company''s privacy policy"]'::jsonb,
 1,
 'CCPA grants California consumers the right to know what personal information businesses collect about them, the right to delete that information, the right to opt out of the sale of their personal information, and the right to non-discrimination for exercising these rights. It does not grant governance, financial disclosure, or policy modification rights.',
 'easy'),

(5, 'Privacy Requirements and Data Protection',
 'HIPAA''s Privacy Rule applies to which of the following entities?',
 '["All businesses that process any type of personal data", "Covered entities (health plans, healthcare providers, clearinghouses) and their business associates", "Only hospitals with more than 500 beds", "Only government healthcare agencies"]'::jsonb,
 1,
 'HIPAA''s Privacy Rule applies to covered entities — health plans, healthcare providers who transmit health information electronically, and healthcare clearinghouses — as well as their business associates who handle protected health information (PHI) on their behalf. It does not apply to all businesses, is not limited by facility size, and includes private sector entities.',
 'easy'),

(5, 'Privacy Requirements and Data Protection',
 'A company collects customer email addresses for order confirmations and then uses them for marketing without additional consent. Under GDPR, this practice most likely violates which principle?',
 '["Data minimization", "Purpose limitation", "Storage limitation", "Accuracy"]'::jsonb,
 1,
 'Purpose limitation (GDPR Article 5(1)(b)) requires that personal data be collected for specified, explicit, and legitimate purposes and not further processed in a manner incompatible with those purposes. Using order confirmation emails for marketing is a different purpose than originally specified, requiring separate consent or another lawful basis.',
 'medium'),

(5, 'Privacy Requirements and Data Protection',
 'Under GDPR, what is a Data Protection Impact Assessment (DPIA) and when is it required?',
 '["A financial audit of data storage costs, required annually", "A risk assessment for data processing activities likely to result in high risk to individuals'' rights, required before such processing begins", "A review of IT infrastructure performance, required quarterly", "A customer satisfaction survey about data handling, required upon request"]'::jsonb,
 1,
 'A DPIA is a risk assessment process that evaluates the impact of proposed data processing activities on individuals'' privacy rights. GDPR Article 35 requires a DPIA before processing that is likely to result in high risk to individuals, such as systematic profiling, large-scale processing of sensitive data, or systematic monitoring of public areas.',
 'medium'),

(5, 'Privacy Requirements and Data Protection',
 'Which of the following techniques would BEST allow an organization to analyze customer purchase patterns while protecting individual privacy?',
 '["Publishing raw transaction data on the company website", "Data anonymization through aggregation and k-anonymity techniques", "Storing data without any access controls", "Emailing individual purchase histories to the marketing team"]'::jsonb,
 1,
 'Data anonymization through aggregation (combining individual records into group statistics) and k-anonymity (ensuring each record is indistinguishable from at least k-1 other records) allows analysis of patterns while preventing identification of individuals. Publishing raw data, removing access controls, and unrestricted sharing all violate privacy principles.',
 'medium'),

(5, 'Privacy Requirements and Data Protection',
 'A multinational company transfers personal data from its EU subsidiary to its US headquarters. Under GDPR, which mechanism is MOST commonly used to legitimize this cross-border transfer?',
 '["Simply notifying data subjects that their data will leave the EU", "Standard Contractual Clauses (SCCs) approved by the European Commission", "Storing an unencrypted copy of the data in both locations", "Obtaining verbal consent from each data subject"]'::jsonb,
 1,
 'Standard Contractual Clauses (SCCs) are pre-approved contractual terms issued by the European Commission that provide appropriate safeguards for transferring personal data from the EU to countries without an adequacy decision. Following the Schrems II ruling, SCCs (often supplemented with additional technical measures) are the most common transfer mechanism. Simple notification, duplicate storage, and verbal consent are insufficient.',
 'medium'),

(5, 'Privacy Requirements and Data Protection',
 'Under the CCPA, a consumer requests that a business delete all personal information collected about them. Which of the following is a valid exception that would allow the business to retain the data?',
 '["The business prefers to keep the data for future marketing campaigns", "The data is needed to complete the transaction for which it was collected", "The business has already backed up the data to tape storage", "The data has been shared with business partners who want to keep it"]'::jsonb,
 1,
 'CCPA provides several exceptions to deletion requests, including when the data is necessary to complete the transaction or provide a requested service, detect security incidents, comply with legal obligations, or engage in research. Business preference for marketing, existence of backups, and third-party wishes are not valid exceptions.',
 'medium'),

(5, 'Privacy Requirements and Data Protection',
 'A healthcare organization experiences a breach affecting 1,000 patient records containing Protected Health Information (PHI). Under HIPAA''s Breach Notification Rule, which entities must be notified?',
 '["Only the affected individuals", "The affected individuals and the U.S. Department of Health and Human Services (HHS)", "The affected individuals, HHS, and prominent media outlets serving the state or jurisdiction", "No notification is required for fewer than 5,000 records"]'::jsonb,
 2,
 'Under HIPAA''s Breach Notification Rule, breaches affecting 500 or more individuals require notification to affected individuals, HHS, and prominent media outlets serving the state or jurisdiction. Since 1,000 records exceeds the 500-person threshold, all three notification obligations apply. Individual notification is required for any breach; HHS and media are required at the 500-person threshold.',
 'hard'),

(5, 'Privacy Requirements and Data Protection',
 'Which privacy principle states that organizations should collect only the minimum amount of personal data necessary for the specified purpose?',
 '["Purpose limitation", "Data minimization", "Storage limitation", "Integrity and confidentiality"]'::jsonb,
 1,
 'Data minimization (GDPR Article 5(1)(c)) requires that personal data be adequate, relevant, and limited to what is necessary in relation to the purposes for which it is processed. This means collecting only the data fields actually needed — for example, not requiring a date of birth for a newsletter subscription that only needs an email address.',
 'easy'),

(5, 'Privacy Requirements and Data Protection',
 'An organization appoints a Data Protection Officer (DPO) as required by GDPR. Which of the following BEST describes the DPO''s role?',
 '["The DPO personally processes all data subject access requests", "The DPO advises the organization on data protection obligations, monitors compliance, and serves as a contact point for supervisory authorities", "The DPO is responsible for IT security operations including firewall management", "The DPO reports to the marketing department to ensure data is maximally utilized"]'::jsonb,
 1,
 'The DPO''s role under GDPR (Articles 37-39) includes advising the organization on data protection obligations, monitoring compliance with GDPR and internal policies, conducting or overseeing DPIAs, cooperating with supervisory authorities, and serving as a contact point for data subjects. The DPO must operate independently and report to the highest level of management, not to marketing or IT operations.',
 'hard'),

(5, 'Privacy Requirements and Data Protection',
 'A company receives a GDPR data subject access request (DSAR). What is the maximum timeframe for responding?',
 '["7 calendar days", "30 calendar days (one month), extendable by two additional months for complex requests", "90 calendar days", "There is no deadline specified in GDPR"]'::jsonb,
 1,
 'GDPR Article 12(3) requires that data controllers respond to DSARs without undue delay and within one month (approximately 30 calendar days) of receipt. For complex or numerous requests, this can be extended by two additional months, but the data subject must be informed of the extension and reasons within the initial one-month period.',
 'hard'),

(5, 'Privacy Requirements and Data Protection',
 'Under PCI DSS, which of the following is a requirement for organizations that store, process, or transmit cardholder data?',
 '["Annual financial audits by a Big Four accounting firm", "Segmentation of the cardholder data environment from the rest of the network", "Publishing cardholder data handling procedures on the company website", "Allowing customers to choose their own encryption algorithms"]'::jsonb,
 1,
 'PCI DSS requires network segmentation to isolate the cardholder data environment (CDE) from other networks, reducing the scope of compliance. While not technically mandatory, segmentation is strongly recommended and practically necessary because without it, the entire network is in scope for PCI DSS compliance — significantly increasing cost and complexity.',
 'hard'),

-- ============================================================
-- Incident Response and Recovery (14 questions)
-- ============================================================

(5, 'Incident Response and Recovery',
 'According to the NIST Computer Security Incident Handling Guide, which phase of incident response comes FIRST?',
 '["Detection and Analysis", "Containment, Eradication, and Recovery", "Preparation", "Post-Incident Activity"]'::jsonb,
 2,
 'NIST SP 800-61 defines four phases of incident response: (1) Preparation, (2) Detection and Analysis, (3) Containment, Eradication, and Recovery, and (4) Post-Incident Activity. Preparation — establishing the incident response capability, policies, procedures, and team — must come first before any incidents can be effectively handled.',
 'easy'),

(5, 'Incident Response and Recovery',
 'During incident response, which activity is part of the "containment" phase?',
 '["Installing antivirus software on all endpoints", "Isolating compromised systems from the network to prevent further spread", "Conducting a tabletop exercise", "Negotiating the incident response team''s budget"]'::jsonb,
 1,
 'Containment involves taking immediate actions to limit the scope and impact of an incident, such as isolating compromised systems from the network, blocking malicious IP addresses, or disabling compromised accounts. The goal is to prevent further damage while the team analyzes and remediates the issue.',
 'easy'),

(5, 'Incident Response and Recovery',
 'A company''s web application is breached, and the incident response team discovers that the attacker gained access through a known vulnerability that should have been patched. Which incident response phase addresses implementing long-term fixes to prevent recurrence?',
 '["Detection", "Containment", "Eradication", "Post-Incident Activity (Lessons Learned)"]'::jsonb,
 3,
 'The Post-Incident Activity phase includes a lessons learned review that identifies root causes and recommends long-term improvements to prevent recurrence — such as improving the patch management process. While eradication removes the immediate threat, post-incident activity addresses the systemic issues that allowed the incident to occur.',
 'medium'),

(5, 'Incident Response and Recovery',
 'An organization''s incident response plan designates a spokesperson for communicating with external parties during a security incident. Which role typically fills this function?',
 '["The junior systems administrator who discovered the incident", "A designated communications or public relations representative, coordinated with legal counsel", "Any available IT staff member", "The external penetration testing vendor"]'::jsonb,
 1,
 'External communication during an incident should be handled by a designated spokesperson — typically from communications or public relations — who coordinates with legal counsel to ensure consistent, accurate, and legally appropriate messaging. Ad hoc communication by technical staff can create legal liability, inconsistent messaging, and public relations problems.',
 'medium'),

(5, 'Incident Response and Recovery',
 'Which of the following is the PRIMARY reason for preserving evidence during incident response?',
 '["To share the details on social media for transparency", "To support potential legal proceedings, regulatory requirements, and root cause analysis", "To create marketing materials about the company''s security capabilities", "To delay the recovery process"]'::jsonb,
 1,
 'Evidence preservation (forensic chain of custody) supports potential legal proceedings (criminal prosecution, civil litigation), regulatory reporting requirements (breach notification), insurance claims, and thorough root cause analysis. Proper evidence handling ensures that digital evidence is admissible and reliable for these purposes.',
 'medium'),

(5, 'Incident Response and Recovery',
 'A ransomware attack encrypts an organization''s accounting database on a Friday night. The most recent clean backup is from Thursday night. The RPO for this system is 4 hours. What is the recovery gap?',
 '["No gap — the Thursday backup meets the RPO", "Approximately 24 hours — the gap between Thursday night backup and Friday night encryption", "4 hours", "The gap cannot be determined without knowing the RTO"]'::jsonb,
 1,
 'The recovery gap is the difference between the actual recovery point (Thursday night backup) and the time of the incident (Friday night). This is approximately 24 hours of data loss, which significantly exceeds the 4-hour RPO. The organization failed to meet its RPO, indicating that backup frequency was insufficient for the defined recovery requirements.',
 'medium'),

(5, 'Incident Response and Recovery',
 'An incident response team determines that an attacker used a compromised service account to access the general ledger database. During the eradication phase, which of the following actions is MOST critical?',
 '["Reboot the database server", "Reset the compromised service account credentials, review all service account access, and close the attack vector", "Send an email notification to all employees", "Purchase new server hardware"]'::jsonb,
 1,
 'Eradication requires removing the attacker''s access and closing the vulnerability that was exploited. Resetting the compromised credentials, reviewing whether other service accounts were affected, and closing the attack vector (e.g., the vulnerability or misconfiguration that allowed the compromise) are the most critical actions. Rebooting, notifications, and new hardware don''t address the root cause.',
 'medium'),

(5, 'Incident Response and Recovery',
 'Which of the following disaster recovery strategies provides the FASTEST recovery time?',
 '["Cold site", "Warm site", "Hot site with real-time replication", "Mobile recovery site"]'::jsonb,
 2,
 'A hot site is a fully operational duplicate of the primary site with real-time data replication, allowing near-instantaneous failover. Warm sites have hardware but need data restoration, cold sites have only basic infrastructure requiring full setup, and mobile sites must be deployed and configured at a location.',
 'easy'),

(5, 'Incident Response and Recovery',
 'An organization conducts a post-incident review after a data breach. Which of the following outcomes is MOST valuable from this review?',
 '["Identifying employees to blame and discipline", "Documenting lessons learned and updating the incident response plan and security controls", "Confirming that the incident was not the IT department''s fault", "Generating a report for the annual shareholder meeting"]'::jsonb,
 1,
 'A post-incident review (lessons learned) should be a blameless analysis focused on improving processes and controls. The most valuable outcomes are documenting what happened, what worked well, what could be improved, and updating the incident response plan and security controls to prevent similar incidents. A blame-focused approach discourages reporting and transparency.',
 'easy'),

(5, 'Incident Response and Recovery',
 'An organization''s business impact analysis (BIA) identifies that its payroll system has a Maximum Tolerable Downtime (MTD) of 48 hours. The current RTO is 72 hours. What does this gap indicate?',
 '["The current recovery capability is adequate", "The organization cannot restore the payroll system within its tolerance for downtime, creating unacceptable business risk", "The payroll system is not important enough to warrant disaster recovery planning", "The MTD should be increased to match the RTO"]'::jsonb,
 1,
 'When the RTO (72 hours) exceeds the MTD (48 hours), the organization cannot restore the system within its maximum tolerable downtime. This means the organization would suffer unacceptable business impacts (missed payroll, regulatory violations, employee hardship) before recovery is complete. The gap must be addressed by improving recovery capabilities to bring the RTO below the MTD.',
 'hard'),

(5, 'Incident Response and Recovery',
 'During a forensic investigation of a compromised financial system, which of the following procedures ensures the integrity of digital evidence?',
 '["Analyzing evidence directly on the compromised production system", "Creating a bit-for-bit forensic image of affected drives and maintaining a documented chain of custody", "Allowing all IT staff to examine the evidence freely", "Restoring from backup immediately before collecting any evidence"]'::jsonb,
 1,
 'Forensic best practices require creating a bit-for-bit image (exact copy) of affected drives to preserve evidence in its original state, then conducting analysis on the copy. A documented chain of custody records who handled the evidence, when, and what actions were taken. Working directly on production systems, unrestricted access, or immediate restoration can destroy evidence.',
 'hard'),

(5, 'Incident Response and Recovery',
 'A company''s incident response team classifies incidents into four severity levels. A severity 1 incident has been declared due to a confirmed data breach of customer financial records. What escalation action is typically required at this severity level?',
 '["The incident is handled by the help desk without escalation", "Executive management, legal counsel, and the board of directors are notified; external counsel and forensic specialists are engaged", "Only the IT manager is informed via email", "The incident is logged for monthly review"]'::jsonb,
 1,
 'A severity 1 incident — a confirmed data breach of customer financial records — requires the highest level of escalation: executive management notification, legal counsel engagement (for breach notification obligations and litigation preparation), board notification (governance requirements), and engagement of external forensic specialists and potentially outside legal counsel for independent investigation.',
 'hard'),

(5, 'Incident Response and Recovery',
 'An organization wants to test its incident response plan without disrupting production systems. Which type of exercise is MOST appropriate?',
 '["Full interruption test that shuts down production systems", "Tabletop exercise where team members walk through a simulated scenario", "Immediately deploying the plan during the next real incident", "Hiring an external red team to launch actual attacks against production", ""]'::jsonb,
 1,
 'A tabletop exercise is a discussion-based exercise where team members walk through a simulated incident scenario, discussing their roles, decisions, and actions. It tests the plan and team readiness without any impact on production systems. Full interruption tests disrupt operations, waiting for real incidents provides no proactive validation, and red team exercises against production carry risk.',
 'medium'),

(5, 'Incident Response and Recovery',
 'After recovering from a ransomware attack, the incident response team must verify that restored systems are clean before reconnecting them to the network. Which procedure is MOST appropriate?',
 '["Reconnect systems immediately to resume business operations as quickly as possible", "Scan restored systems with updated anti-malware tools, verify file integrity, and test in an isolated environment before reconnecting to the production network", "Ask the attacker for confirmation that the malware has been removed", "Replace all hardware with new equipment regardless of cost"]'::jsonb,
 1,
 'Before reconnecting restored systems, they should be scanned for residual malware with updated signatures, file integrity should be verified against known-good baselines, and systems should be tested in an isolated environment to confirm they are clean. Reconnecting without verification risks reinfection, attacker communication is unreliable, and wholesale hardware replacement is unnecessary and wasteful.',
 'hard'),

-- ============================================================
-- SOC Engagements (12 questions)
-- ============================================================

(5, 'SOC Engagements',
 'What does SOC stand for in the context of System and Organization Controls reports?',
 '["Security Operations Center", "System and Organization Controls", "Standards of Compliance", "Service Organization Certification"]'::jsonb,
 1,
 'In the context of assurance reports, SOC stands for System and Organization Controls. These are reports issued by independent auditors that examine a service organization''s controls. While "Security Operations Center" also uses the SOC acronym, in the assurance/audit context, SOC refers to the AICPA''s reporting framework.',
 'easy'),

(5, 'SOC Engagements',
 'Which type of SOC report is designed for user entities and their auditors to evaluate the effect of a service organization''s controls on the user entity''s financial statements?',
 '["SOC 1", "SOC 2", "SOC 3", "SOC for Cybersecurity"]'::jsonb,
 0,
 'SOC 1 reports (formerly SAS 70) evaluate controls at a service organization that are relevant to user entities'' internal control over financial reporting (ICFR). User auditors rely on SOC 1 reports to understand how the service organization''s controls affect their client''s financial statement assertions. SOC 2 covers trust services criteria, and SOC 3 is a general-use report.',
 'easy'),

(5, 'SOC Engagements',
 'A cloud hosting provider that processes payroll for 200 client companies wants to demonstrate its security and availability controls to prospective customers through a publicly available report. Which SOC report type is most appropriate?',
 '["SOC 1 Type II", "SOC 2 Type II", "SOC 3", "SOC for Supply Chain"]'::jsonb,
 2,
 'SOC 3 is a general-use report that can be freely distributed and even published on a service organization''s website. It provides a summary-level assurance opinion on the trust services criteria (security, availability, etc.) without the detailed control descriptions and test results found in SOC 2. SOC 1 and SOC 2 reports are restricted-use documents.',
 'medium'),

(5, 'SOC Engagements',
 'What is the key difference between a SOC 2 Type I and a SOC 2 Type II report?',
 '["Type I covers financial controls; Type II covers operational controls", "Type I evaluates control design at a point in time; Type II evaluates both design and operating effectiveness over a period", "Type I is more rigorous than Type II", "Type I covers security only; Type II covers all five trust services criteria"]'::jsonb,
 1,
 'SOC 2 Type I evaluates the suitability of the design of controls at a specific point in time. SOC 2 Type II evaluates both the design suitability AND the operating effectiveness of controls over a specified period (typically 6-12 months). Type II provides more assurance because it tests whether controls actually operated as designed over time.',
 'medium'),

(5, 'SOC Engagements',
 'During a SOC 2 engagement, the service auditor identifies a control deficiency where access reviews were not performed for three of the twelve months in the examination period. How should this be reported?',
 '["It should be omitted from the report because it was eventually corrected", "It should be described in the auditor''s description of tests and results, including the nature of the deficiency and the period during which it occurred", "It should only be reported if the client agrees to include it", "It should be reported in a separate management letter, not in the SOC 2 report"]'::jsonb,
 1,
 'In a SOC 2 Type II report, the auditor must describe all tests performed and their results, including any control deficiencies identified. The three-month gap in access reviews must be reported in the description of tests of controls and results section, identifying the control, the testing performed, and the exception or deviation found.',
 'medium'),

(5, 'SOC Engagements',
 'A user auditor is reviewing a SOC 1 Type II report for a payroll service organization. The report includes "Complementary User Entity Controls" (CUECs). What is the user auditor''s responsibility regarding CUECs?',
 '["CUECs are informational only and require no action", "The user auditor must evaluate whether the user entity has implemented the CUECs that are relevant to their audit", "The service auditor is responsible for testing CUECs", "CUECs only apply to SOC 2 reports"]'::jsonb,
 1,
 'CUECs are controls that the service organization assumes the user entity will implement. The service organization''s controls are designed to be effective only in combination with these user controls. The user auditor must evaluate whether their client has implemented the relevant CUECs, as the SOC 1 opinion is predicated on their existence.',
 'hard'),

(5, 'SOC Engagements',
 'Which of the following professionals is qualified to perform a SOC engagement?',
 '["Any CPA with a public accounting license", "A CPA or CPA firm that meets the requirements of AT-C Section 320 (reporting on an examination of controls) and relevant AICPA standards", "Any IT security professional with a CISSP certification", "An internal auditor employed by the service organization"]'::jsonb,
 1,
 'SOC engagements are attestation engagements that must be performed by a CPA or CPA firm in accordance with AICPA attestation standards (AT-C 205 for SOC 2/3, SSAE 18 AT-C 320 for SOC 1). The practitioner must be independent and meet competency requirements. CISSP holders and internal auditors cannot issue SOC reports.',
 'medium'),

(5, 'SOC Engagements',
 'A service organization receives a qualified opinion on its SOC 2 Type II report. What does this indicate?',
 '["The controls are perfectly designed and operating effectively", "The auditor identified one or more material exceptions or deviations from the trust services criteria that are significant but not pervasive", "The service organization failed all control objectives", "The report should not be distributed to any user entities"]'::jsonb,
 1,
 'A qualified opinion indicates that, except for the specific matters described, the controls were suitably designed and operating effectively. The exceptions are significant enough to warrant qualification but not so pervasive as to warrant an adverse opinion (which would indicate fundamental control failures). User entities should review the specific qualifications to assess their impact.',
 'hard'),

(5, 'SOC Engagements',
 'Management of a service organization asserts in its SOC 1 report that controls were suitably designed and operated effectively during the examination period. This assertion is known as:',
 '["The auditor''s opinion", "Management''s assertion (or management''s description and assertion)", "The user entity''s representation", "The regulatory compliance certificate"]'::jsonb,
 1,
 'In a SOC 1 engagement, management of the service organization provides a written assertion that its description of the system is fairly presented and that controls were suitably designed and, for Type II, operated effectively during the period. The auditor then examines this assertion and issues an independent opinion on its fairness.',
 'medium'),

(5, 'SOC Engagements',
 'An organization uses a cloud provider that subcontracts data storage to a third party. In the SOC 2 report, how should the subservice organization be addressed?',
 '["The subservice organization is always excluded from the scope", "Either through the inclusive method (included in scope) or the carve-out method (excluded with a description of the nature of services)", "Subservice organizations are only relevant in SOC 1 reports", "The user entity is responsible for auditing the subservice organization"]'::jsonb,
 1,
 'SOC reports address subservice organizations through either the inclusive method (the subservice organization''s controls are included in the scope and tested) or the carve-out method (the subservice organization is excluded from scope, and the report describes the nature of services provided and the controls expected of the subservice organization). Both methods are valid approaches.',
 'hard'),

(5, 'SOC Engagements',
 'How frequently should a service organization obtain a SOC 2 Type II report to maintain continuous assurance for its user entities?',
 '["Every five years", "Annually, covering a 12-month examination period with no gaps between report periods", "Only when requested by a user entity", "Monthly"]'::jsonb,
 1,
 'Best practice is to obtain SOC 2 Type II reports annually, with each report covering a 12-month period that aligns with or closely follows the prior report period to avoid coverage gaps. User auditors rely on current reports; a gap between report periods means user auditors cannot rely on controls during the uncovered period.',
 'medium'),

(5, 'SOC Engagements',
 'A service organization is preparing for its first SOC 2 engagement. The examination period has not yet begun. Which type of report can be issued immediately?',
 '["SOC 2 Type II — the auditor can test controls retroactively", "SOC 2 Type I — which evaluates control design at a point in time", "SOC 3 — which does not require an examination period", "No SOC report can be issued until the examination period is complete"]'::jsonb,
 1,
 'A SOC 2 Type I report evaluates the suitability of control design at a specific point in time (a snapshot date) and does not require an examination period over which to test operating effectiveness. This allows a service organization to obtain a Type I report immediately, demonstrating control design while building a track record for a subsequent Type II report.',
 'hard'),

-- ============================================================
-- SOC Reporting and Trust Services Criteria (12 questions)
-- ============================================================

(5, 'SOC Reporting and Trust Services Criteria',
 'The AICPA Trust Services Criteria (TSC) include five categories. Which of the following is the foundational category that must be included in every SOC 2 engagement?',
 '["Availability", "Confidentiality", "Security (Common Criteria)", "Privacy"]'::jsonb,
 2,
 'Security (also known as the Common Criteria) is the foundational category that must be included in every SOC 2 engagement. It addresses protection of information and systems against unauthorized access, unauthorized disclosure, and damage. The other four categories — Availability, Processing Integrity, Confidentiality, and Privacy — are optional and selected based on the service organization''s commitments.',
 'easy'),

(5, 'SOC Reporting and Trust Services Criteria',
 'Which Trust Services Criteria category addresses whether a system processes transactions in a complete, valid, accurate, timely, and authorized manner?',
 '["Security", "Availability", "Processing Integrity", "Confidentiality"]'::jsonb,
 2,
 'Processing Integrity addresses whether system processing is complete, valid, accurate, timely, and authorized. It ensures that transactions are processed correctly and that outputs are reliable. This is particularly relevant for service organizations that process financial transactions on behalf of user entities.',
 'easy'),

(5, 'SOC Reporting and Trust Services Criteria',
 'A SOC 2 report includes the Availability criteria. Which of the following controls would be MOST relevant to this category?',
 '["Background checks for new employees", "Redundant data center infrastructure with automated failover and defined SLAs for uptime", "Encryption of data at rest", "Physical access controls to the server room"]'::jsonb,
 1,
 'The Availability criteria address whether the system is operational and usable as committed. Redundant infrastructure with automated failover and defined SLAs directly supports availability by ensuring the system can withstand component failures and meet uptime commitments. Background checks relate to security, encryption to confidentiality, and physical access to security.',
 'medium'),

(5, 'SOC Reporting and Trust Services Criteria',
 'Which of the following BEST describes the Confidentiality criteria in the Trust Services framework?',
 '["Ensuring that personal information is collected and used in compliance with privacy laws", "Protecting information designated as confidential from unauthorized disclosure", "Ensuring that systems are available for operation and use", "Ensuring that processing is complete, valid, and accurate"]'::jsonb,
 1,
 'The Confidentiality criteria address the protection of information designated as confidential — such as business plans, intellectual property, or client data marked confidential — from unauthorized disclosure. This differs from Privacy, which specifically addresses personal information, and from Security, which broadly covers unauthorized access to all information.',
 'medium'),

(5, 'SOC Reporting and Trust Services Criteria',
 'The Privacy criteria in the Trust Services framework align with which widely recognized privacy framework?',
 '["ISO 27001", "NIST CSF", "AICPA Generally Accepted Privacy Principles (GAPP)", "COBIT 2019"]'::jsonb,
 2,
 'The Privacy criteria in the Trust Services framework align with the AICPA''s Generally Accepted Privacy Principles (GAPP), which cover notice, choice and consent, collection, use and retention, access, disclosure, security, and quality. ISO 27001 covers information security, NIST CSF covers cybersecurity, and COBIT covers IT governance.',
 'medium'),

(5, 'SOC Reporting and Trust Services Criteria',
 'A SOC 2 report describes a control as follows: "The entity restricts logical access to information assets using role-based access controls." Under which Trust Services Criteria category does this control MOST directly fall?',
 '["Availability", "Processing Integrity", "Security (Common Criteria)", "Privacy"]'::jsonb,
 2,
 'Logical access controls that restrict access to information assets based on roles fall under the Security (Common Criteria) category. Security controls protect against unauthorized access and form the foundation of the Trust Services framework. While access controls may support other categories, restricting logical access is fundamentally a security control.',
 'easy'),

(5, 'SOC Reporting and Trust Services Criteria',
 'A service organization''s SOC 2 report covers Security and Availability criteria. A user entity''s auditor needs assurance about Processing Integrity. What should the user auditor do?',
 '["Accept the existing report as sufficient — Security and Availability imply Processing Integrity", "Request that the service organization include Processing Integrity in the next SOC 2 report, or perform additional audit procedures independently", "Issue a qualified opinion on the user entity''s financial statements", "Ignore the gap because SOC 2 reports are voluntary"]'::jsonb,
 1,
 'If the user auditor needs assurance about Processing Integrity but the SOC 2 report only covers Security and Availability, the auditor must obtain evidence through other means — either requesting the service organization include Processing Integrity in the next report, performing independent audit procedures, or obtaining other evidence. Coverage of one criterion does not imply another.',
 'hard'),

(5, 'SOC Reporting and Trust Services Criteria',
 'Which of the following points of focus under the Security criteria relates to evaluating how an organization communicates its commitments and system requirements to external users?',
 '["CC1.1 — COSO Principle 1: Demonstrates Commitment to Integrity and Ethical Values", "CC2.3 — The entity communicates with external parties regarding matters affecting the functioning of internal control", "CC5.1 — COSO Principle 10: Selects and develops control activities", "CC3.1 — COSO Principle 6: Specifies suitable objectives"]'::jsonb,
 1,
 'CC2.3 addresses communication with external parties about matters that affect the functioning of internal controls, including communicating commitments, system requirements, and responsibilities to users, business partners, vendors, and regulators. This ensures external stakeholders understand the service organization''s obligations and their own responsibilities.',
 'hard'),

(5, 'SOC Reporting and Trust Services Criteria',
 'In a SOC 2 report, the "System Description" section is prepared by:',
 '["The service auditor", "Management of the service organization", "The user entity''s auditor", "The AICPA"]'::jsonb,
 1,
 'Management of the service organization is responsible for preparing the system description, which details the system''s components, boundaries, commitments, and relevant controls. The service auditor examines the description and provides an opinion on whether it is fairly presented, but management owns and prepares the description.',
 'medium'),

(5, 'SOC Reporting and Trust Services Criteria',
 'Which of the following items must be included in the system description of a SOC 2 report?',
 '["The service organization''s annual revenue", "The types of services provided, system boundaries, principal service commitments, and relevant aspects of the control environment", "The personal salaries of IT staff", "The service organization''s marketing strategy"]'::jsonb,
 1,
 'The system description in a SOC 2 report must include the types of services provided, system components and boundaries, principal service commitments and system requirements, relevant aspects of the control environment, risk assessment process, information and communication systems, monitoring activities, and control activities. Financial performance, individual salaries, and marketing strategies are not relevant.',
 'medium'),

(5, 'SOC Reporting and Trust Services Criteria',
 'An organization''s SOC 2 report is qualified because monitoring controls over third-party access were not operating effectively for four months of the twelve-month period. A user auditor relying on this report should:',
 '["Disregard the qualification because it covers only one-third of the period", "Evaluate the impact of the qualification on their audit, test compensating controls at the user entity, and potentially expand substantive procedures for the affected period", "Automatically issue a qualified opinion on the user entity''s financial statements", "Request the service organization to reissue the report without the qualification"]'::jsonb,
 1,
 'The user auditor must evaluate the significance of the qualification to their audit. This includes understanding the nature and duration of the control failure, assessing whether the user entity had compensating controls, and potentially expanding substantive audit procedures for the four-month period when monitoring controls were not effective. A qualification does not automatically result in a qualified opinion for the user entity.',
 'hard'),

(5, 'SOC Reporting and Trust Services Criteria',
 'The SOC for Cybersecurity framework differs from SOC 2 in that it:',
 '["Uses different attestation standards", "Provides an entity-wide cybersecurity risk management examination rather than focusing on a specific system or service", "Is only available to government agencies", "Does not require a CPA to perform the examination"]'::jsonb,
 1,
 'SOC for Cybersecurity provides an entity-wide examination of the organization''s cybersecurity risk management program, whereas SOC 2 focuses on a specific system or service. SOC for Cybersecurity evaluates the organization''s overall cybersecurity posture and risk management processes across all systems, not just those related to a specific service offering.',
 'hard');
